  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cool and dry weather continues through the weekend,\nalthough a slow-moving Nor'easter off the coast will bring gusty\nwinds along the Chesapeake Bay and minor tidal flooding at times\nthrough Friday.\n\nThe position of the Nor'easter hasn't changed much from\nyesterday at this time, a few hundred miles east-southeast of\nCape Hatteras. Strong high pressure builds toward Quebec tonight\ninto Tuesday, while the low starts to drift northward as the \npreceding northern stream shortwave causes a weakness in the \nblock. This will lead to a greater pressure gradient and \nslightly breezier conditions Tuesday. For tonight, that also \nmeans fewer areas will become calm. In addition, dew points will\nbe higher where the growing season continues, so frost appears \nunlikely tonight for most areas. However, a small area of the\nnorth-central Virginia piedmont (US-29/US-15 corridor from\nManassas to Warrenton to Culpeper to Orange) is experiencing\nrapidly falling temperatures, slightly lower dew points, and may\nbe more sheltered from light winds later tonight. Have hoisted a\nsmall Frost Advisory for these areas. Some patchy frost is\npossible elsewhere, but should be more localized.\n\nThe ridge will continue to expand north and west of the low,\nblocking it from substantial movement through mid week. Although\nsome uncertainty remains in the exact track/timing of the \nNor'easter, this pattern will keep the low far enough off the \ncoast to eliminate rain potential (and clouds for that matter) \nacross the forecast area. Overall, any impacts will be tied to \ncoastal communities along the Chesapeake Bay where potential \nminor coastal flooding and gusty marine winds remain possible \nconcerns this week. More information on those topics is included\nin sections below.\n\nTemperatures will climb slightly through mid week, ending up on\neither side of normal values.\n\nBy Thursday, what remains of the upper reflection of the offshore \nclosed low gradually becomes absorbed into a deep trough over \neastern Canada. These adjoining features are slated to pull eastward \ninto the Canadian Maritimes while upstream heights build across the \nMississippi Valley. From Friday and into this weekend, this broad \nridge axis advances eastward ahead of a trough inhabiting the \nwestern/central U.S. As this occurs, there are some hints of another \nupper low center getting pinched off near the Carolina coast. \nHowever, its far southward displacement should minimize any direct \nimpacts from this system. Overall synoptic uncertainty does increase \nmarkedly toward the end of the weekend and into early next week. \nMost notably, it revolves around a split in the jet streams over the \nmiddle of the continent. \n\nLooking down at the surface, a fresh area of high pressure\nbehind the departing trough/Canadian cold front dominates \nthrough late week while becoming more centered over the \nnortheastern U.S. by Saturday. This ensures continued tranquil \nweather heading toward the last week of October. Expect dry \nconditions along with abundant sunshine each day. The air mass \nwill remain quite dry owing to the prevailing northerly \ncomponent to the local wind fields. Most days should yield highs\nin the 60s (50s for the mountains). The coldest overnight \nperiods will likely be Friday and Saturday nights as lows fall \ninto the 30s to low 40s. With the growing season having ended \nfrom the Blue Ridge westward, it remains to be seen if any \nfrost/freeze threats emerge off to the east.",
